WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.670
Film Courage: So your debut novel The Thirteenth
Continuum sold and then at some point now

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:10.959
you have a three-book deal (which that was
the first book of the three) and it’s being

00:00:10.959 --> 00:00:11.959
turned into a film?

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:13.009
Jennifer Brody, Author: Yeah, we’re working
on that.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:18.100
So my background is that I used to work in
Film and TV development.

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:23.390
And actually before I even sold it to a publisher,
I had producers that were working on it.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:27.410
It has always had a lot of interest from a
lot of producers.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.580
The first set of producers wanted to do it
for TV and we were sort of working for them.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:35.390
But at a certain point they weren’t being
super effective and we kind of felt like going

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:41.150
more in the film direction, so I switched
to a different producer and she’s been great.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:43.750
We’re working on packing it into a film.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.510
So they have a deal at Warner Brothers.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:51.250
And then now we’re looking at writers and
directors because in the environment of right

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:55.880
now it really helps to have a package and
to go in with a package and there are a couple

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.150
of reasons why I think that’s good.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:03.110
But one of them is that I don’t want to
rush and do a bad version of the film.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:07.220
I’ve worked enough in Hollywood and worked
on enough big book adaptations like LORD OF

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:12.790
THE RINGS and the GOLDEN COMPASS that I know
it really matters how you put it together.

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.820
And also, it’s one thing just to option
a book.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:20.530
You can do that but then there is something
we call development hell where it just kind

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.260
of gets stuck and it never gets made.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:26.490
So the more you have attached and the more
you go in with, the more likely it is that

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.280
you’re going to be actually able to make
the film because I don’t just want to option

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:35.549
it and have it sit there are so many fabulous
books and famously popular books that have

00:01:35.549 --> 00:01:39.400
never been made that sold for a lot a money
that studios have.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.070
Things like Justin Cronin’s The Passage
is a good example and it just hasn’t been

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:44.070
made.

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.670
It’s been years and years.

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:49.670
So I kind of want to do it in a way that really
does it justice so I’m not really in a rush,

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:50.780
I want to do it right.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.760
Film Courage: Well so the second book is coming
out November 1st?

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:54.760
[Editor’s note: it’s currently available].

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:55.760
Jennifer Brody: Yes.

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:56.760
Film Courage: And tell me the title again?

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.180
Jennifer Brody: It’s called Return of the
Continuums.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.450
Film Courage: And then you have a third book
which you’re finishing up now as we speak.

00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:03.450
Jennifer Brody: Yes.

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.479
I am finishing it up now and that will come
out in probably the end of May next year and

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:09.380
it’s called The United Continuums.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:13.170
So that’s the other things that’s nice
about the series is that with each book is

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:17.780
an opportunity and it really kind of drives
attention and drives readership and there’s

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:22.010
a whole marketing effort, so it’s kind of
nice because you don’t have one book and

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:26.099
that kind of gets forgotten and you’re left
behind each time you’re going out with a

00:02:26.099 --> 00:02:29.950
new book it’s bringing them all kind of
together and you see a lot of times these

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.260
things build over time.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:37.220
Readership builds, popularity builds and we
can all cite really famous series where they

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.040
weren’t big until later.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:44.042
There’s a great piece by the author, he
wrote Percy Jackson series [Rick Riordan]

00:02:44.042 --> 00:02:46.300
which were made into films.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.870
And I think he said it was two full years
after the first book came out that they first

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.200
had an inkling that it was going to be a huge
hit.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.430
But for two years it wasn’t selling much
and they weren’t sure.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.310
He was doing signings and nobody was showing
up but it was a building process, like they

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:05.300
really believed in it and they really worked
hard.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.590
So it can take time.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.840
And the other thing to remember with the book
and I’m sort of experiencing this now is

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.801
that it’s not like a film where everyone
will go opening day and see it and the be

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:18.260
talking about it to their friends.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:21.940
Even if people buy it close to when it comes
out, it might not get read instantly.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:25.629
A lot of us (and this includes me) have a
ton of books in our queue and a lot of things.

00:03:25.629 --> 00:03:27.019
And it doesn’t mean that we’re not going
to read it.

00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:31.650
But it might be a month or two later that
I finally do and so you start to see that

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.340
even a couple months after it’s been out
people are finally like “Oh my gosh, I finally

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.340
read this.”

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:39.150
And you start to hear and all of the sudden
more people start to hear.

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:44.011
So it’s not like an opening day phenomenon
and the other cool thing about books is they

00:03:44.011 --> 00:03:47.110
exists and once they’re in the world, they’re
in the world a long time.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:50.370
So you start to see them popping up in libraries
and popping up.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:54.930
That’s the nice thing about Young Adult
is that libraries are huge for us.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:55.989
Schools are huge for us.

00:03:55.989 --> 00:03:57.799
School libraries are huge for us.

00:03:57.799 --> 00:04:00.909
And there also a lot of kids that can’t
really afford or don’t have the budgets

00:04:00.909 --> 00:04:04.880
to buy books so they’re waiting to get them
from their library, which I think is fantastic.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:06.500
I was that kid, so I totally understand that.

